<section class="non-normative">
## Introduction ## {#introduction}

*This section is non-normative.*

A WebExtension can declare a [=/service worker=] as its
<a href="https://w3c.github.io/webextensions/specification/#key-background">background</a>
context. Browsers make some capabilities available in that worker that are
not available in other [=/service workers=]. This is done where user
permission is better granted at a different level in the extension permission
model than the web offers, such as hardware access.

This specification does not define the lifetime of an
[=extension service worker=], and it does not revisit the processing model of
[=/service workers=].

This specification defines [=extension service workers=] and the
[=extension service worker realm=], so that WebIDL can expose a construct in
[=extension service workers=] without exposing it in other [=/service workers=].

</section>

## Conformance of user agents ## {#conformance-of-user-agents}

The requirements in this specification are on user agents that implement
[[SERVICE-WORKERS]] and support WebExtensions. They are stated in
[[#exposure-condition]] and [[#security]].

## Extension service workers ## {#extension-service-workers}

The <dfn export lt="extension service worker|background service worker">extension
service worker</dfn> of a WebExtension is the [=active worker=] of that
WebExtension's [=background service worker registration=]. The extension service
worker is a [=privileged extension context=].

An [=extension service worker=] is a [=secure context=], as
[[SERVICE-WORKERS]] requires, because [[webextensions]] makes each
[=extension scheme=] a scheme the user agent considers to be authenticated in
the sense of [[SECURE-CONTEXTS]].

## The extension service worker realm ## {#exposure-condition}

A [=realm=] is an <dfn export>extension service worker realm</dfn> if it was
created for a [=/service worker=] of a
[=background service worker registration=].

Note: An [=extension service worker realm=] is defined by the registration
the realm was created for, rather than by the realm's eventual
[=global object=], because WebIDL decides exposure while the realm's initial
objects are being created, before [[SERVICE-WORKERS]] associates the
[=global object=] with its [=/service worker=].

The [=extension service worker realm=] is meant to be used as a condition
on the `ServiceWorker` entry of a construct's [=exposure set=] in the
[{{Exposed}}] extended attribute. The construct is then [=exposed=] in the
[=extension service worker realm=] and not in other [=/service workers=].
Entries for other [=global names=] are unaffected. A construct whose
[=exposure set=] names `Worker`, which covers every worker global, lists
`DedicatedWorker` and `ServiceWorker` separately so that the condition can be
placed on the latter alone.

A user agent must not treat the presence of a construct in a [=realm=] as
authorization to use the capability it provides. The extension's permissions
are checked on each use, independently of exposure, as described in
[[#security]].

Issue: WebIDL does not currently allow a condition on an individual entry of
an [=exposure set=]. Its exposure conditions ([{{SecureContext}}] and
[{{CrossOriginIsolated}}]) apply to a whole construct. This requires a
proposal to WebIDL. Until WebIDL accepts a mechanism, no specification can
reference the [=extension service worker realm=] from IDL.

Issue: [[webextensions-browser-global]] declares
`partial interface ServiceWorkerGlobalScope { attribute Browser browser; }`,
which, read literally, exposes {{Browser}} in every [=/service worker=]. The
`ServiceWorker` entry of that partial's [=exposure set=] should be limited to
the [=extension service worker realm=] once WebIDL supports it.

## What the owning specification defines ## {#owning-spec-defines}

A specification that limits a construct's `ServiceWorker` exposure to the
[=extension service worker realm=] defines the following.

: The exposure.
:: The constructs whose `ServiceWorker` exposure is limited to the
    [=extension service worker realm=], including every `partial interface`
    or member through which the construct is reached, such as a member added
    to {{WorkerNavigator}}. If the construct
    is also meant to be available in the extension's windows or dedicated
    workers, those [=global names=] appear in the same declarations.

: User-facing operations.
:: For any operation that presents a chooser, a prompt, or other user
    interface, or that requires [=transient activation=]: whether it stays
    `[Exposed=Window]`, and if it is exposed in the worker, what gates it there.

: Document assumptions.
:: For every algorithm step that consults [=transient activation=], the
    [=relevant global object=]'s associated {{Document}}, a
    [=browsing context=], or a top-level origin, what happens when the
    [=relevant global object=] is that of an [=extension service worker=],
    which has none of these.

: The permission.
:: Whether it invokes the [=extension permission check=] and, if so, which
    permission it passes.

: Lifetime.
:: Whether, and how, pending work keeps the worker alive and whether any
    event starts it, using {{ExtendableEvent}} and [=functional events=] from
    [[SERVICE-WORKERS]], without assuming the worker stays alive longer than
    [[SERVICE-WORKERS]] allows.

## Security considerations ## {#security}

Exposing a construct in an [=extension service worker=] gives extension code
headless access to a capability that the web gates on one or more of a
document, a user gesture, and a prompt. The extension permission model of
[[webextensions]] takes the place of those gates: the user consents when the
permission is granted, at install time or at run time, and can withdraw it.

Where an algorithm of the owning specification would prompt, consult
[=transient activation=], or evaluate a document's permissions policy, in an
[=extension service worker=] it instead runs the
[=extension permission check=] for the feature. A prompt is never shown from
an [=extension service worker=], and [[PERMISSIONS-POLICY]], which defines no
policy for a [=/service worker=]'s settings object, is not consulted.

The user agent must run the [=extension permission check=] on each use of the
capability, so that a permission revoked while the worker is running takes
effect on the next use.

The user agent must make use of such a capability from an
[=extension service worker=] attributable to the extension in its user
interface, since the tab-level indicators that accompany the capability in a
window are not available.

Whether an [=extension service worker=] runs in a private browsing session,
and with which granted permissions, is governed by [[webextensions]].

## Privacy considerations ## {#privacy}

This specification exposes nothing to web content. An
[=extension service worker realm=] belongs only to an
[=extension service worker=], whose script already runs with the extension's
permissions. What the headless path removes is the user
gesture and the visible surface that accompany the same capability in a
window, which is why [[#security]] requires use to be attributable to the
extension.

